+## 7. Closing — what JD should be prepared for, ranked
+
+⛔⛔ **METHOD NOTE, STATED SO THE RANKING IS NOT MISTAKEN FOR A PREDICTION.** The ordering below reflects **(i)** how much each argument would have to do to move anything Rev. James has already conceded, and **(ii)** how much evidential machinery it needs. ⛔⛔⛔ **It is NOT a claim about what Rev. James will say, will raise, or would endorse. He has never been shown this article and nothing in the corpus connects him to it.** Every entry naming a corpus finding names it as an already-established record, per `CLAUDE.md` attribution discipline.
+
+### 7.1 The three concessions that frame everything below
+
+- **`IP-111`** — in his own voice, unprompted, on the 1899 Opinion: *"I'm not aware of an explicit reference of positive usage of incense in the first, say, three centuries,"* preceded by *"Yeah, I'm fine with that argument."* ⛔ **The entry's own guard binds: this is a statement about HIS OWN KNOWLEDGE, not about the historical record, it is NOT a contradiction and NOT a retraction, and `DQ-27`(b)'s fork is NOT closed by it.**
+- **`DQ-27`** — the five-level framework applied to incense: (1) Biblical, (2) Traditional (*"used all throughout Church History"* — ⚠️ **ambiguity recorded and unresolved** between *continuously and everywhere* and *present somewhere at most times*), (3) Established Custom, (4) Bishop, (5) Rector. ⚠️ **`DQ-27`(f): the church-wide/jurisdictional axis question is asked twice and answered zero times.**
+- **`DQ-28`** — levels (1)–(3) **allow** incense and do **not demand** it; the binding decision arises at (4) and (5). ⭐⭐⭐ **Affirmed in his own word — *"Correct."* — to JD's explicit proposition, ⛔ NOT inferred from silence.** ⚠️ **`DQ-28`(d)'s wording guard: the affirmation must never be quoted detached from the 4:01 PM question it answers.**
+
+### 7.2 ⭐⭐⭐ The observation that outranks the ranking
+
+⭐⭐⭐ **THE ARTICLE ARGUES FOR A STRONGER POSITION THAN `DQ-28` RECORDS REV. JAMES HOLDING, AND THAT GAP IS THE MOST USEFUL THING ON THIS PAGE.**
+
+Arakaki's conclusion is not that incense is **permitted**. It is that incense is *"a marker of right worship"*; that Malachi **predicted** it for the Messianic age; that the pattern *"began to unravel with the Protestant Reformation"*; and that for Protestants who *"feel they have lost their way, incense can serve as a marker guiding them back to the ancient paths."* ⭐ **That is a normative claim about what worship ought to contain.**
+
+⛔⛔ **`DQ-28`(c)/(d) records Rev. James, asked directly, affirming that levels (1)–(3) allow incense and do not demand it — and affirming *"Correct."* to the proposition that *"a parish that never used incense at all wouldn't be failing at levels 1 through 3 the way they would if they never read Scripture."*** ⭐⭐ **`DQ-28`(e) records that this is his own narrowing of his own framework, obtained by a structural question — ⛔ "not a concession extracted, and it must never be characterised outward as one."**
+
+⭐⭐⭐ **SO THE ARTICLE AND `DQ-28` DO NOT SIT AT THE SAME STRENGTH.** ⛔⛔ **Nothing follows from that about Rev. James, and this document asserts nothing.** ⭐ **What it does supply is the shape of the single most useful question if the article is ever produced in conversation: not whether its history is right, but what work it is being asked to do.** At full strength — incense as a marker of right worship whose absence is a loss — it says more than levels (1)–(3) have been said to say. At the strength compatible with `DQ-28`, it establishes permission, **which is not in dispute between the parties.**
+
+⛔⛔⛔ **KEEP BOTH READINGS LIVE AND ASK WHICH IS MEANT.** `CLAUDE.md`: *"If his reasoning is genuinely ambiguous between two readings, keep both readings live and ask which he means — don't pick one and ask him to ratify it."* ⛔ **Do not frame this as an inconsistency. It is a question about what the article is for.**
+
+⚠️ **AND THE ADJACENT GUARD IS RESTATED:** `DQ-28`(e) records `IP-118`(b) — *"If I were Pope of the entire Church, I would say the expectation is that we have incense"* — as **compatible on its face** with the permissive half and expressly **not adjudicated**. ⛔ **That non-adjudication is untouched here and nothing in §7.2 may be read as disturbing it.**
+
+### 7.3 The ranking
+
+**1. ⭐⭐⭐ The context filter (CA-2) — near-certain, and it should be conceded in one breath.**
+It needs no new evidence, it is the natural reply to *any* patristic citation JD offers, and it is `260835-47` §3's own finding. ⭐ **Concede it first and completely.** The only step available afterwards is `260835-47` §2's distinction between the polemical *occasion* and the stated *reason* — one question, then stop. **Preparation owed: none. It is already in the repo.**
+
+**2. ⭐⭐⭐ The universality / prevalence argument (CA-11) — high, and it is where JD's material is strongest.**
+It maps onto `BLOG-121`'s burden rule and onto `DQ-27`(b)'s unresolved ambiguity, and the answer comes entirely from the pro-incense side's own paper: Frere's first-three-centuries concession, the *"disproving so far as they go"* clause, the Levitical-precedent limb, and Geldart dating the English extinction to 1550–1552. ⛔⛔ **Three binding guards: never say "Vincentian" (absolute zero in his corpus); never produce `BLOG-121` as an inconsistency charge (its own guard); and treat Frere as relocating the argument, not winning it (`Ritualist_Case` §2g's warning).** **Preparation owed: none.**
+
+**3. ⛔⛔ The ancient liturgies (CA-6) — high, and it is the only thing in the article that would move `IP-111`.**
+`IP-111` is a concession about *explicit positive usage in the first three centuries*. The Liturgy of St James is precisely the counter-instance such a concession invites, and the Addai and Mari **rubrics** are the strongest form of it. ⚠️ **But the likely form is weaker than Arakaki's**: `BLOG-53` records Rev. James's own dating at *"at absolute latest"* the fourth century, three centuries later than the article. ⛔⛔ **`BLOG-53` is record-only and must never be produced at him.** ⭐ **Frere is the answer: liturgical-book evidence c. 700–800; altar-censing "one of the later forms."** ⏳ **Preparation owed: read the three liturgies in ANF 7 and establish the dating position for stratum vs. redaction. Not done.**
+
+**4. ⛔⛔ Eusebius, *DE* 1.10 (CA-4) — medium likelihood, highest unpreparedness.**
+It is the article's only clean-looking positive patristic citation, it is quotable in one line, and **JD has nothing on it.** ⚠️ The article's own excerpts contain the material for a metaphorical reading (*DE* 1.6 glosses Malachi's incense as *"the incense of prayer"*; the 1.10 passage itself says *"offering it in our prayers to Him"*), and Frere reported the patristic handling of Mal. 1:11 as prayer-or-silence. ⛔ **None of that is a verdict.** ⏳⏳ **Preparation owed, and it is the single highest-value unfinished item on this page: read *Demonstratio Evangelica* 1.10 in full at Tier 1.** ⭐ Until then the honest answer is that he hasn't read the chapter — which is true, and better than a guess.
+
+**5. ⚠️ *Apostolic Canons* Canon 3 (CA-7) — medium-low.**
+Fully answered by `260835-49` Task 2, and the article's handling is weaker than Brattston's (no dating dispute; Trullo 692 offered as though it argued for earliness; the Hippolytus contradiction conceded rather than met). ⭐ Ask what date he takes it to bear before discussing what it says. **Preparation owed: none, though the canon's text is unverified.**
+
+**6. ⚠️ Source-weighting (CA-1) — low-medium, and it is self-harming.**
+Discounting Tertullian and Origen leaves the two strongest of the eight (Athenagoras, Justin *1 Apol.* 13) untouched and discards the two passages that most help the pro-incense side. ⭐ Ask whether the filter applies symmetrically. **Preparation owed: none.**
+
+**7. ✅ The Old Testament / Malachi case (CA-8, CA-15) — certain to come up, but not *from this article*.**
+It is already `DQ-27`(a) in his own voice, better put than Arakaki puts it, and the project has a dedicated survey document. ⭐ Nothing here changes the state of that argument. ⛔ **`IP-98` remains cross-referenced-not-adjudicated and is not to be produced as a gotcha.** ⏳ **Minor item owed: Irenaeus *AH* 4.17, unverified, and the article's gloss exceeds its own quotation.**
+
+**8. ⚠️ *Lex orandi, lex credendi* (CA-12) — low from this article, but it is the article's honest core and it is unanswered.**
+Having conceded that no Father addresses Christian ceremonial incense, the article's real argument for the practice is the practice. ⛔ **Nothing in the repo engages this**, and it belongs to `DQ-24`/`DQ-25`/`DQ-26` rather than to patristics. ⭐ Recorded as live and unanswered, not as weak. **Preparation owed: none for this conversation; it is a larger question.**
+
+**9. ⛔ The silence argument, the Gnostic complaint, the Malachi 1:33 typo, the lawyer jab, the retraction call (CA-5, CA-9, CA-10, CA-12's ad hominem, CA-14) — very low, and four of the five should simply be conceded or declined.**
+⭐ **CA-5 is the exception and is worth having ready**, because the enumerated-silence principle is one JD is already committed to symmetrically (`Ritualist_Case` §7d(1)(a), where it *costs* him). ⛔ **The other four are not arguments about incense and answering them in kind would be a mistake.**
+
+### 7.4 ⭐⭐⭐ The one thing to have in the front of the mind
+
+⭐⭐⭐ **ON THIS ARTICLE'S CENTRAL CONCLUSION — *"not a single shred of evidence of an early church father objecting to the use of incense in Christian worship"* — ARAKAKI IS RIGHT, AND THE REPO ESTABLISHED HE WAS RIGHT BEFORE READING HIM, AGAINST ITS OWN INTEREST** (`260835-47` §3 and §5). ⭐⭐ **Granting that sentence flatly, first, and without qualification costs nothing and buys the position that makes it affordable: what the eight texts establish is the narrower proposition that the ante-Nicene Fathers rejected incense as a *sacrificial oblation*, on theological grounds — which the sentence does not touch.** ⛔ **Anything less than a clean concession reads as evasion.**
